===Etymology 1===
Probably a {{glossary|back-formation}} from {{m|en|screes}}, from {{der|en|non|skriða||landslide, landslip}}; compare {{m|non|skríða||to glide}}<ref>{{R:Oxford Dictionaries Online}}</ref> (from {{der|en|gem-pro|*skrīþaną||to crawl; to glide; to walk}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*(s)kreyt-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*(s)ker-||to bend, turn}}). The word is cognate with {{cog|is|skriða||avalanche; landslide, landslip; steep mountain- or hillside made up of gravel and loose rocks}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[loose#Adjective|Loose]] [[stony]] [[debris]] on a [[slope#Noun|slope]]. {{defdate|from early 18th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Bing (Francis J.) West|authorlink=Bing West|chapter=XXV|title=The Pepperdogs: A Novel|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Simon & Schuster}}|year=2003|page=328|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=TXgvyQAoxngC&pg=PA328|isbn=978-0-7432-3803-8|passage=Sprawled prone among the rocks, the team waited for a reply to their request to fire. {{...}} Occasional rounds zinged off the '''scree''', each with a different pitch.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|by extension}} Similar debris made up of [[broken]] [[building]] [[material#Noun|material]] such as [[brick#Noun|bricks]], [[concrete#Noun|concrete]], etc.
#* {{quote-book|author=Catherine Merridale|authorlink=Catherine Merridale|title=Night of Stone: Death and Memory in Twentieth-century Russia|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Penguin Books}}|year=2002|page=243|oclc=637663692|passage=Acres of the industrial port had been reduced to desolation, half-walls, half-chimneys, crazy sticks of steel that looped up out of concrete '''scree'''. The temptation not to clear and rebuild must have been strong.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A slope made up of loose stony debris at the [[base#Noun|base]] of a [[cliff]], [[mountain]], etc.
#* {{quote-book|author=Michael J. Sallnow|title=Pilgrims of the Andes: Regional Cults in Cusco|series=Smithsonian Series in Ethnographic Inquiry|location=Washington, D.C.; London|publisher=[[w:Smithsonian Institution|Smithsonian Institution Press]]|year=1987|page=189|isbn=978-0-87474-826-0|passage=The next landmark was an apachita 'cairn', at the top of a steep '''scree'''. Each pilgrim carried a stone to the summit, spat on it, and threw it on to the cairn — the purpose being, I was told, to relieve the soul of its sins {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|author=I[oannis] N[ikolaou] Vogiatzakis|author2=O. Rackham|chapter=Crete|editors=I. N. Vogiatzakis, G[loria] Pungetti, and A[ntoinette] M. Mannion|title=Mediterranean Island Landscapes: Natural and Cultural Approaches|series=Landscape Series|seriesvolume=9|location=Dordrecht|publisher={{w|Springer Science+Business Media}}|year=2008|section=section II (Mediterranean Island Landscapes)|page=252|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Ij4qWj3dXRoC&pg=PA252|isbn=978-1-4020-5063-3|passage=Also, special to Crete, and probably derived from some aspect of its climactic history, is cementation. Many '''screes''' are converted, in whole or in part, into concrete-like breccias. Carapaces of cemented '''scree''' encrust steep slopes. Cliffs of conglomerate or marl are covered with a layer of re-deposited limestone and look like hard rock. However, the loose contents are apt to trickle out through a breach in the crust, resulting in the jagged and hollow cliffs which are a picturesque feature of Crete}}

